[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from the Luna Ultra. It has a slightly wider viewing angle that seems more natural, whereas the X6 sometimes has the look of an action camera. When Insta360 first released the Luna Ultra, I found the colors in the i-log profile'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to be a bit distorted. I thought the skin tones were n\'t very good. They were giving off a little green or yellow, but I feel like they fixed that with numerous firmware updates. I think the colors are pretty accurate now, and I'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also think that on the X6, when I first took it out of the box, the colors in I-Log looked fantastic right away. Honestly, my favorite way to shoot with these cameras is at their highest resolution in I-Log format. I think'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this provides the best image quality. And as for these resolutions and frame rates, this is where things get a little confusing because you\'re working with two very different cameras. Both of them can actually record in 8K. So,'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Luna Ultra can record in 8K at up to 30 frames per second. So there is no possibility of slow motion. You can only shoot at standard 30, 25, or 24 frames per second in 8K mode.'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But the X6, on the other hand, can also record in 8K, but now at up to 50 frames per second. The X5 could only output 30fps in 8K, and the X6 can now do 50fps. So now you'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can slow down the footage to about 50 % of its speed if you have a 24 frames per second timeline, which is pretty cool. But the caveat with 8K is that you don\'t output an 8K project from the X6 because it'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
